   MMeessssaaggee SSiiggnnaalleedd IInntteerrrruuppttss
     Message Signaled Interrupts (MSI) and Enhanced Message Signaled Inter‐
     rupts (MSI-X) are PCI capabilities that provide an alternate method for
     PCI devices to signal interrupts.  The legacy INTx interrupt is available
     to PCI devices as a SYS_RES_IRQ resource with a resource ID of zero.  MSI
     and MSI-X interrupts are available to PCI devices as one or more
     SYS_RES_IRQ resources with resource IDs greater than zero.  A driver must
     ask the PCI bus to allocate MSI or MSI-X interrupts using ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssii()
     or ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssiixx() before it can use MSI or MSI-X SYS_RES_IRQ resources.
     A driver is not allowed to use the legacy INTx SYS_RES_IRQ resource if
     MSI or MSI-X interrupts have been allocated, and attempts to allocate MSI
     or MSI-X interrupts will fail if the driver is currently using the legacy
     INTx SYS_RES_IRQ resource.  A driver is only allowed to use either MSI or
     MSI-X, but not both.

     The ppccii__mmssii__ccoouunntt() function returns the maximum number of MSI messages
     supported by the device _d_e_v.  If the device does not support MSI, then
     ppccii__mmssii__ccoouunntt() returns zero.

     The ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssii() function attempts to allocate _*_c_o_u_n_t MSI messages for
     the device _d_e_v.  The ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssii() function may allocate fewer messages
     than requested for various reasons including requests for more messages
     than the device _d_e_v supports, or if the system has a shortage of avail‐
     able MSI messages.  On success, _*_c_o_u_n_t is set to the number of messages
     allocated and ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssii() returns zero.  The SYS_RES_IRQ resources
     for the allocated messages will be available at consecutive resource IDs
     beginning with one.  If ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssii() is not able to allocate any mes‐
     sages, it returns an error.  Note that MSI only supports message counts
     that are powers of two; requests to allocate a non-power of two count of
     messages will fail.

     The ppccii__rreelleeaassee__mmssii() function is used to release any allocated MSI or
     MSI-X messages back to the system.  If any MSI or MSI-X SYS_RES_IRQ re‐
     sources are allocated by the driver or have a configured interrupt han‐
     dler, this function will fail with EBUSY.  The ppccii__rreelleeaassee__mmssii() function
     returns zero on success and an error on failure.

     The ppccii__mmssiixx__ccoouunntt() function returns the maximum number of MSI-X mes‐
     sages supported by the device _d_e_v.  If the device does not support MSI-X,
     then ppccii__mmssiixx__ccoouunntt() returns zero.

     The ppccii__mmssiixx__ppbbaa__bbaarr() function returns the offset in configuration space
     of the Base Address Register (BAR) containing the MSI-X Pending Bit Array
     (PBA) for device _d_e_v.  The returned value can be used as the resource ID
     with bus_alloc_resource(9) and bus_release_resource(9) to allocate the
     BAR.  If the device does not support MSI-X, then ppccii__mmssiixx__ppbbaa__bbaarr() re‐
     turns -1.

     The ppccii__mmssiixx__ttaabbllee__bbaarr() function returns the offset in configuration
     space of the BAR containing the MSI-X vector table for device _d_e_v.  The
     returned value can be used as the resource ID with bus_alloc_resource(9)
     and bus_release_resource(9) to allocate the BAR.  If the device does not
     support MSI-X, then ppccii__mmssiixx__ttaabbllee__bbaarr() returns -1.

     The ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssiixx() function attempts to allocate _*_c_o_u_n_t MSI-X messages
     for the device _d_e_v.  The ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssiixx() function may allocate fewer
     messages than requested for various reasons including requests for more
     messages than the device _d_e_v supports, or if the system has a shortage of
     available MSI-X messages.  On success, _*_c_o_u_n_t is set to the number of
     messages allocated and ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssiixx() returns zero.  For MSI-X mes‐
     sages, the resource ID for each SYS_RES_IRQ resource identifies the index
     in the MSI-X table of the corresponding message.  A resource ID of one
     maps to the first index of the MSI-X table; a resource ID two identifies
     the second index in the table, etc.  The ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssiixx() function as‐
     signs the _*_c_o_u_n_t messages allocated to the first _*_c_o_u_n_t table indices.
     If ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssiixx() is not able to allocate any messages, it returns an
     error.  Unlike MSI, MSI-X does not require message counts that are powers
     of two.

     The BARs containing the MSI-X vector table and PBA must be allocated via
     bus_alloc_resource(9) before calling ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssiixx() and must not be re‐
     leased until after calling ppccii__rreelleeaassee__mmssii().  Note that the vector table
     and PBA may be stored in the same BAR or in different BARs.

     The ppccii__ppeennddiinngg__mmssiixx() function examines the _d_e_v device's PBA to deter‐
     mine the pending status of the MSI-X message at table index _i_n_d_e_x.  If
     the indicated message is pending, this function returns a non-zero value;
     otherwise, it returns zero.  Passing an invalid _i_n_d_e_x to this function
     will result in undefined behavior.

     As mentioned in the description of ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssiixx(), MSI-X messages are
     initially assigned to the first N table entries.  A driver may use a dif‐
     ferent distribution of available messages to table entries via the
     ppccii__rreemmaapp__mmssiixx() function.  Note that this function must be called after
     a successful call to ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssiixx() but before any of the SYS_RES_IRQ
     resources are allocated.  The ppccii__rreemmaapp__mmssiixx() function returns zero on
     success, or an error on failure.

     The _v_e_c_t_o_r_s array should contain _c_o_u_n_t message vectors.  The array maps
     directly to the MSI-X table in that the first entry in the array speci‐
     fies the message used for the first entry in the MSI-X table, the second
     entry in the array corresponds to the second entry in the MSI-X table,
     etc.  The vector value in each array index can either be zero to indicate
     that no message should be assigned to the corresponding MSI-X table en‐
     try, or it can be a number from one to N (where N is the count returned
     from the previous call to ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssiixx()) to indicate which of the al‐
     located messages should be assigned to the corresponding MSI-X table en‐
     try.

     If ppccii__rreemmaapp__mmssiixx() succeeds, each MSI-X table entry with a non-zero vec‐
     tor will have an associated SYS_RES_IRQ resource whose resource ID corre‐
     sponds to the table index as described above for ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssiixx().  MSI-X
     table entries that with a vector of zero will not have an associated
     SYS_RES_IRQ resource.  Additionally, if any of the original messages al‐
     located by ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssiixx() are not used in the new distribution of mes‐
     sages in the MSI-X table, they will be released automatically.  Note that
     if a driver wishes to use fewer messages than were allocated by
     ppccii__aalllloocc__mmssiixx(), the driver must use a single, contiguous range of mes‐
     sages beginning with one in the new distribution.  The ppccii__rreemmaapp__mmssiixx()
     function will fail if this condition is not met.

